  <article>
    <section>
      <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>
      <p>Firstly create a directory and enter it with the following commands:</p>
      {% render 'organism/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'mkdir my-static-site', type: 'console' %}
      {% render 'organism/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'cd my-static-site', type: 'console' %}
      <p>Or create one by hand!</p>

      {% render 'organism/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_init', cmd: 'init', managers: managers %}

      <p>Then get the following packages ready:</p>

      {% render 'organism/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_liquidjs', cmd: 'add liquidjs', managers: managers %}
      {% render 'organism/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_11ty', cmd: 'add -D @11ty/eleventy', managers: managers %}
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 id="11ty">Configuring Eleventy (v3+)</h2>
      <p>
        Modern Eleventy relies on ES Modules (ESM) by default. Create an explicit configuration file named
        <span class="code">elventy.config.js</span>
        in your project root and add the modern
        <span class="code">export default</span> structure:
      </p>

      <pre><code>export default function (eleventyConfig) {
  return {
    dir: {
      input: "src",
      output: "_site"
    }
  };
};</code></pre>

      <p>
        This explicitly tells Eleventy to process everything inside your
        <span class="code">src</span> directory and compile the optimized, production-ready static assets directly into
        <span class="code">_site</span>.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 id="templating">Your First Template</h2>
      <p>Create your source folder structure and your primary template file:</p>
      {% render 'organism/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'mkdir src', type: 'console' %}
      {% render 'organism/CodeBlock/CodeBlock', cmd: 'touch src/index.liquid', type: 'console' %}

      <p>
        Open up the <span class="code">src/index.liquid</span> file and add a front matter block along with a dynamic
        Liquid array loop to test parsing capabilities:
      </p>
      <pre><code>&lt;---
title: Welcome to Liquify
---&gt;
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;&#123;&#123; title &#125;&#125;&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;&#123;&#123; title &#125;&#125;&lt;/h1&gt;
  
  &#123;% assign array = "HTML, CSS, Liquid" | split: ", " %&#125;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &#123;% for item in array %&#125;
      &lt;li&gt;Rendering: &#123;&#123; item &#125;&#125;&lt;/li&gt;
    &#123;% endfor %&#125;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 id="fire-up">Fire Up the Build Server</h2>
      <p>
        To keep development moving quickly, add a start/dev script execution path directly into your
        <span class="code">package.json</span> file:
      </p>
      <pre><code>"scripts": {
    "dev": "eleventy --serve"
  }</code></pre>

      <p>
        Now, run the script from your terminal using your active workflow package manager to boot up the hot-reloading
        development server:
      </p>

      {% render 'organism/PackageTab/PackageTab', name: 'pkg_run', cmd: 'dev', managers: managers %}

      <p>
        The Eleventy compiler will now build your site and launch a hot-reloading dashboard locally at
        <span class="code highlight-block">http://localhost:8080</span>. Open it up and start hacking!
      </p>
    </section>
